---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import CaseStudyHero from '../components/CaseStudyHero.astro';

const APP = 'https://fitscript.emilydenis.com';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="FitScript Case Study | Emily Denis"
  description="An AI training coach that builds your race plan, keeps you improving between races, and adjusts each day to how you slept and how you feel. Rules make the training calls and the AI explains them. PM case study by Emily Denis."
  ogImage="/images/fitscript-dashboard.png"
>
  <Header backLink />

  <CaseStudyHero
    title="FitScript"
    tagline="Case study · AI personalized guidance"
    intro="An AI training coach that builds a plan for your race, keeps you improving between races, and adjusts each day's workout to how you slept and how you feel. Rules make every training call. The AI explains the call in plain language and writes the workouts, and everything it writes is checked before you see it."
    facts={[
      { label: 'Role', value: 'PM · design · engineering (solo)' },
      { label: 'Status', value: 'Live, in daily use' },
      { label: 'Stack', value: 'React · Supabase · Claude' },
      { label: 'Quality bar', value: '16/16 eval scenarios' },
    ]}
    ctas={[
      { label: 'Try the live demo (no signup)', href: `${APP}/demo`, primary: true },
      { label: 'Open the app', href: APP },
    ]}
  />

  <article class="prose-custom mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-24">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      The motivated but constrained athlete trains for real races while juggling a
      job, a family, and interrupted sleep. They own a wearable and they care about
      doing the right workout, but nobody has time to weigh readiness scores, a
      training plan, and a race calendar at 6am. The data exists; the
      <strong>synthesis</strong> is the missing product.
    </p>
    <p>
      Generic plans break on contact with real life. A bad night turns today's
      interval session into a liability, and a plan that can't bend gets
      abandoned. FitScript's premise is that <strong>the plan should bend, not
      break.</strong>
    </p>

    <h2>Version one: one good answer a day</h2>
    <p>
      The first version answered a single question each morning: what should I do
      today? A one-tap check-in, plus sleep, HRV, and the race calendar, went to
      an AI coach that suggested one workout and explained why in my own numbers.
      An eval suite gated every change to the coach, and a rules engine stepped in
      whenever the AI was unavailable.
    </p>
    <p>
      It was good at single days. Then I used it for a whole training block.
    </p>

    <h2>What using it taught me</h2>
    <p>
      After the Chicago Marathon I signed up for the Big Apple Half and decided
      FitScript would be my only plan. I stopped paying for a separate training
      plan app and trained off my own product every day. Three things became
      obvious within a couple of weeks.
    </p>

    <h3>A good day is not a plan</h3>
    <p>
      A coach that picks one workout at a time has no memory of the block. It
      can't build a long run week over week, protect a taper, or tell a skipped
      long run from a planned rest day. I needed a real plan underneath the daily
      advice, with the daily advice adjusting that plan instead of replacing it.
    </p>

    <h3>The AI shouldn't make the safety calls</h3>
    <p>
      How fast mileage climbs, how many days you run, and when you rest are
      decisions with real injury risk. I didn't want those to depend on how a
      model read the situation on a given morning. The model is very good at
      explaining a decision kindly and clearly, and at writing a workout that
      feels like mine. So I split the job: <strong>rules decide, the AI
      explains.</strong>
    </p>

    <h3>Real use finds the bugs tests don't</h3>
    <p>Every one of these came from my own training, not a test suite:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Six run days and no strength.</strong> My first generated plan
        had me running six days a week with no strength work. I'd been running
        four. The form was reading my profile's training days, which include
        strength days, as run days. I fixed the form and added a guard so the
        plan can never ask for much more running than you've actually been doing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A swap that didn't stick.</strong> One morning I didn't have
        time for my long run, so I swapped it with tomorrow's rest day. The plan
        updated, but the Today card still said long run. The swap had saved while
        today's card was being rebuilt from the old plan. Now today's decision
        checks that it's still about the session on today's date, and repairs
        itself if not.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Miles that never counted.</strong> The coach told me to "keep
        going after the class until you hit 6 miles," but the class app I use
        stops recording when a class ends, so those miles never reached Strava.
        Now a workout built from classes has to add up to the full session, and
        any that doesn't is rejected before I see it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How it works now</h2>

    <h3>A plan built from your actual running</h3>
    <p>
      You give FitScript a race, an optional goal time, and how many days you can
      run and lift. It reads your recent running from Strava (or your log) and
      suggests your fitness numbers, including your last training block, so
      coming back after a race doesn't mean starting from zero. You confirm or
      correct them, and a deterministic generator builds the block: phases,
      cut-back weeks, a long run that grows safely, and a taper. The same inputs
      always produce the same plan, which makes it testable.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you enter a goal time, the plan checks it against your recent races and
      runs. It supports ambitious goals and builds goal-pace work for them. If a
      goal isn't safely reachable in the time you have, it says so kindly and
      suggests a target that is.
    </p>

    <h3>Each morning, the rules make the call</h3>
    <p>
      The check-in asks four quick things: energy, soreness, anything hurting,
      and whether you're sick. Together with your wearable (HRV against your own
      30-day average, sleep, temperature), that gives a green, amber, or red
      day. A rules table maps the day and the session to an action: keep it,
      shorten it, swap it with an easier day, move it, or rest. The AI then
      writes the reason in plain language. If the AI is slow or unavailable, the
      rules' own explanation stands, so the call never waits on the model.
    </p>

    <figure class="mt-8">
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/images/fitscript-checkin-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
        <img src="/images/fitscript-checkin.png" alt="FitScript's morning check-in: energy and soreness on a 1 to 5 scale, plus something hurts and I'm sick" class="card w-full" loading="lazy" />
      </picture>
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-zinc-400">Four quick answers, asked once each morning.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="mt-8">
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/images/fitscript-today-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
        <img src="/images/fitscript-today.png" alt="Today's workout card: the planned 10 mile long run crossed out, today's version is 8 miles all easy, with the reason citing HRV 18% under the 30-day average, and buttons for Sounds good and Do it as planned" class="card w-full" loading="lazy" />
      </picture>
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-zinc-400">An amber morning: the rules shorten the long run, and the coach explains why. "Do it as planned" is always one tap away.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>The AI writes the workouts, and every one is checked</h3>
    <p>
      For each session, the AI writes the actual workout in the way you like to
      train, outdoors or in classes, with notes that fit the day. Before
      anything is saved, a validator checks each one against the plan. The
      distance can't go over the target, a lineup of classes has to add up to
      the session, an easy day can't turn into a hills workout, and no pace can
      be faster than your goal pace. A workout that fails keeps the plain
      template instead. The database enforces the
      same line: the AI can rewrite a session's text, never its date, type, or
      distance.
    </p>
    <figure class="mt-8">
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/images/fitscript-plan-weeks-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
        <img src="/images/fitscript-plan-weeks.png" alt="The training plan by day: finished sessions marked done with what was actually run, today's key long run highlighted, and a panel moving next week's interval session to Wednesday with a summary of what changes" class="card w-full" loading="lazy" />
      </picture>
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-zinc-400">The plan by day, with the coach's workouts, what actually happened, and moving a workout. Moves follow the same spacing rules as the plan.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>What actually happened</h3>
    <p>
      Workouts from Strava or Oura are matched to the plan, so each session shows
      done, partial, or missed, along with adherence, a streak, and whether
      you're on track for your goal. Miss a key session and FitScript asks the
      same day whether to move it or let it go. Miss enough of the plan and it
      offers to rebuild from today, keeping the old version as history.
    </p>
    <figure class="mt-8">
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/images/fitscript-progress-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
        <img src="/images/fitscript-progress.png" alt="Progress card: 86% adherence, a 9 day streak, on track, a bar chart of miles run against miles planned for each week, and long run progress" class="card w-full" loading="lazy" />
      </picture>
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-zinc-400">Planned against actual, week by week, with a table view of the same numbers.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>After the workout: a debrief, not a checkbox</h3>
    <p>
      The first time I marked a run done in my own app, the button turned
      green and that was it. It was anticlimactic. The moment right after a
      workout is when you most want to know how it went and whether it
      mattered, so "Mark as done" now opens a short debrief: how hard it felt,
      how your legs feel, anything that hurt, and an optional note.
    </p>
    <p>
      What comes back is split the same way as the morning call. Code compares
      the run with its target (distance, time, and pace from Strava), judges
      the effort against what the session was for, and says what it did for
      your training: the week so far, whether it was the key session, where
      the next long run goes from here, and how many days are left to the
      race. The AI adds two or three sentences responding to what you said.
      Strava's terms don't allow its data to go to AI models, so the coach
      note is written from your feedback and the plan only, and a check
      throws out any note that cites a pace or a distance it wasn't given.
    </p>
    <p>
      The debrief also feeds the next morning. An easy run that felt hard, or
      legs that feel heavy, counts as one signal in tomorrow's readiness, and
      feeling strong when you wake up can still outvote it.
    </p>

    <h3>Your plan, wherever you are</h3>
    <p>
      I check my calendar and talk to Claude far more often than I open a
      training app, so the plan goes to both. A private subscription link puts
      it in Google Calendar, Apple Calendar, or Outlook as all-day events that
      never block your time, and a session the rules changed this morning is
      marked "changed today."
    </p>
    <p>
      A Claude connector lets you ask what today's workout is, check in, or
      move a session from a conversation. It runs on the same rules as the app:
      Claude can explain a decision and make the changes you ask for, but it
      can't invent a session or overrule the plan, and every change goes through
      the same spacing checks as a move in the app. Strava's terms don't allow
      its data to go to AI models, so the connector shares the plan and your
      check-ins only. Both links are private, and you can replace or turn off
      either one from Settings.
    </p>

    <h2>The next question: what happens after race day?</h2>
    <p>
      Writing the plan engine raised a question I hadn't designed for. The day
      after the Big Apple Half, I'd have no race on the calendar, and FitScript
      would quietly fall back to version one: one AI-picked workout a day, with
      no plan, no progress, and none of the rules above. The goals people set in
      Settings (strength, endurance, general fitness, weight loss) weren't
      shaping any plan at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      I wrote the fix up as a spec before building it, with the open decisions
      listed at the end. The core idea is that <strong>every athlete always has
      exactly one plan in effect</strong>: a race plan while a race block is
      under way, a short recovery block right after a race, and otherwise an
      ongoing plan built from their goals in 4-week cycles. Everything
      downstream already ran off a plan, so the morning check-in, the rules
      table, "Do it as planned," progress, and the calendar all work the same
      with or without a race.
    </p>

    <h3>Holding steady wasn't what I wanted</h3>
    <p>
      My first draft had volume build to a comfortable ceiling between races
      and hold there. Reading it back as the athlete, I didn't want to hold. I
      wanted to keep getting better, and "better" without a race needs a reason
      for each block and a way to see it. So the default became
      <strong>keep improving</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Base cycles</strong> build volume gently, about 5% a week, back
        toward your last race block and then a little past it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sharpen cycles</strong> hold volume steady while the hard
        sessions progress: one more interval each week, a little more tempo.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A 5K time trial every 8 weeks</strong> answers "am I improving?"
        You log your time, and it sets the next cycle's paces and shows as a
        trend. A local 5K race counts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A time goal with no date</strong>, like a faster 5K, is optional.
        Speed work trains toward it as fast as is safe from your latest result,
        and once it's in reach, FitScript suggests finding a race.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      "Maintain" stays available in Settings for busy stretches.
    </p>
    <figure class="mt-8">
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/images/fitscript-ongoing-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
        <img src="/images/fitscript-ongoing.png" alt="The ongoing plan card: cycle 2, week 4 of a sharpen cycle, built from endurance plus strength goals, a time goal of 23:45 marked ambitious and doable, time trials showing a 5K of 24:41 then 24:02, and progress with 95% adherence, a 16 day streak, consistency on track, and miles run against planned by week" class="card w-full" loading="lazy" />
      </picture>
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-zinc-400">An example ongoing plan in its benchmark week: the goals it was built from, a dateless time goal, the time trial trend, and consistency in place of a race goal.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>Goals that actually shape the week</h3>
    <p>
      Each goal now maps to a weekly mix. An endurance week has a long session,
      easy days, one quality session, and strength. A strength week has three
      rotating full-body sessions whose sets and reps progress across the
      cycle. For weight loss, the emphasis is consistency and easy aerobic time,
      with at most one hard day a week and no nutrition advice anywhere. Target
      intensity caps the hard days: none, one, or two. People who don't run get
      cardio in the activities they actually like, so the plan isn't only for
      runners.
    </p>

    <h3>Rules for a new kind of session</h3>
    <p>
      A time trial needed its own row in the rules table. On a rough morning,
      an easy run is still worth doing, but an easy time trial measures nothing.
      So a time trial on an amber or red day moves to a later day that week,
      and if there isn't one, it waits for the next cycle.
    </p>
    <figure class="mt-8">
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/images/fitscript-time-trial-dark.png" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
        <img src="/images/fitscript-time-trial.png" alt="The cut-back week of a sharpen cycle: strength at 2 sets of 10, a 5K time trial marked done with the logged result of 24:02, easy runs, and a 7.5 mile long run on Saturday" class="card w-full" loading="lazy" />
      </picture>
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-zinc-400">The time trial lands in the lighter fourth week. Logging the result sets the next cycle's paces.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Product decisions</h2>

    <h3>1 · Rules decide, the AI explains</h3>
    <p>
      Splitting the job made both halves better. The training logic is
      deterministic, so I can test it and reason about it. The AI does what it's
      genuinely good at: explaining a decision warmly, in your numbers, and
      making the workout feel like yours. When the two disagree, the rules win.
    </p>

    <h3>2 · Feel is first-class data, within limits</h3>
    <p>
      In version one, the coach always followed the more cautious signal. Using
      it changed my mind. If my ring said my HRV was down but I felt great, the
      app kept dialing back a workout I was ready for. Now feeling strong can
      outvote a single off signal from the wearable. It can never outvote pain
      or illness.
    </p>

    <h3>3 · You always get the last word</h3>
    <p>
      Every adjustment comes with "Do it as planned." It's never blocked or
      second-guessed, just logged, so the plan can learn from what you actually
      chose. A coach you can't overrule is a coach you stop opening.
    </p>

    <h3>4 · Guardrails instead of vigilance</h3>
    <p>
      I'd rather the product make an unsafe plan impossible than count on someone
      noticing it. The plan can't ask for much more running than you've been
      doing. Coming back after a race, volume climbs back toward your old base on
      a capped ramp. And if you ask for more strength days than fit around your
      runs, light upper body and core work rides along after an easy run instead
      of stealing a rest day.
    </p>

    <h3>5 · Ship what I've used</h3>
    <p>
      The race plan and the daily calls went through my own training first.
      That's where the three bugs above came from, and it's why I trust the
      fixes. The ongoing plan is the exception for now: it's live, and its first
      real test is the morning after the Big Apple Half, when my account should
      move from the race plan to a recovery week to my first base cycle without
      me doing anything.
    </p>

    <h3>6 · Write the spec before the code</h3>
    <p>
      The ongoing plan touched the generator, the database, every screen that
      shows a plan, and the AI's instructions. I wrote it up first, with six
      open decisions at the end, and answered them before building. One of my
      answers changed the design: I asked what happens if I want to keep
      improving without a race, which is how "keep improving" became the
      default.
    </p>

    <h2>The quality bar</h2>
    <p>
      Personalized guidance with safety stakes shouldn't ship on gut feel, and I
      don't trust myself to eyeball AI quality. A suite of 16 golden scenarios
      runs against both the rules engine and the deployed coach: no intensity on
      a fever signal, a real taper in race week, no heroics the day after a
      race, and real training on good days. Early on, the suite caught the rules
      engine prescribing a moderate workout during a fever signal, before anyone
      relied on it. The deployed coach currently passes all 16.
    </p>
    <p>
      The plan engine has its own unit tests (256 and counting), and a separate
      eval runs the AI's workouts through the same validator the app uses.
      The ongoing plan added a third eval with realistic athletes: a strength
      goal, weight loss at high intensity (never more than one hard day), low
      intensity (no hard days at all), the week after a half marathon, and a
      time trial on a bad morning. Coach changes ship only when the evals pass.
    </p>

    <h2>Under the hood</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>React, Vite, and Tailwind on the front end; Supabase for Postgres and auth, with row-level security on every table.</li>
      <li>The plan engine is plain TypeScript with no I/O: the race and ongoing generators, the readiness bands, the rules table, rescheduling, and matching actuals to the plan. One shared function decides which plan is in effect on any day, for the app and the server alike.</li>
      <li>The coach is Claude (Opus 5.5) with structured outputs, so every answer parses. It explains decisions, writes the workouts, and handles chat.</li>
      <li>Data from Oura, Strava, and an Apple Health relay, matched across sources so the same run never counts twice.</li>
      <li>The Claude connector is a small MCP server running as a serverless function, with seven tools (today, the plan, check in, preview and confirm a move, undo, do as planned). The calendar is a standard iCalendar feed generated from the same plan.</li>
      <li>The zero-signup demo runs the real plan engine and the live coach entirely in memory. Nothing you do there is saved.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="mt-12 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a href={`${APP}/demo`} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn-primary">Try the live demo ↗</a>
      <a href="/" class="btn-secondary">← All work</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
